{"conjecture": "Software systems degrade not through the accumulation of bugs, but through the systematic under-execution of deletion: each locally correct change adds state and invariants while preserving obsolete ones, causing the ratio of live invariants to live functionality to grow asymptotically. Because reviewers and tests validate additions but rarely penalize residual complexity, the system's effective design surface area expands monotonically, increasing the probability that any future change intersects an unstated, cross-cutting dependency. The system becomes harder to change because the cost of verifying a change scales with the total number of accumulated invariants, not the complexity of the change itself."}
